This argument routinely comes up over any sort of long-lived game,
especially one where next to none of the players were present at the
game's inception.  Having had this argument before over this and several
other games in the past, I feel the need to weigh in here and clarify
terminology.

In general, the point of _playing_ any game is to have fun.

However, it's important not to confuse the point of playing in the first
place with the object of the game itself, and the stated victory
conditions for the game.

The object of the game is to retrieve the Amulet of Yendor for your god.
The victory condition is to offer the amulet on your god's altar.

It is quite possible to play the game with an individual alternate
victory condition, such as "successfully perform the protection racket";
and it is also quite possible to have fun while doing so.  But it's 
important to be clear here that the actions taken are under an
_alternate_ victory condition, not the one laid out by the game's
designers.

General statements should always be considered to refer to the primary
victory condition unless specifically declared to refer to an
individual or group's alternate victory condition.  From this, when 
trying to determine whether someone is "good" (no qualifier) at the game, 
one would obviously target that player's ascension count and efficiency 
thereof.  If one wanted to determine whether someone is "good at the 
protection racket", though, ascensions would have little bearing on this.

For most players, "winning" and "having fun" are largely synonymous,
which leads to confusion when discussions like this come up.  Much of
that confusion is from terminology... hence the paragraphs above.

